WEBVTT

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:06.887
<v Ishita>Today there are clocks everywhere: on the wall, on my wrist...

00:00:06.887 --> 00:00:14.552
<v Mishu>A pendulum clock, a wristwatch, a digital watch, and a stopwatch, to time an athlete in a hundred-metre race.

00:00:14.552 --> 00:00:17.450
<v Rohan>Which clock is the most accurate of all?

00:00:17.450 --> 00:00:22.849
<v Mishu>The atomic clock. Today, the second itself is defined using atomic clocks.

00:00:22.849 --> 00:00:25.703
<v Ishita>So how many seconds are in an hour?

00:00:25.703 --> 00:00:30.728
<v Mishu>Climb the ladder with me. Sixty seconds in a minute. Sixty minutes in an hour.

00:00:30.628 --> 00:00:32.564
<v Mishu>Twenty-four hours in a day.

00:00:32.464 --> 00:00:36.212
<v Mishu>Three hundred and sixty-five and a quarter days in a year.

00:00:36.212 --> 00:00:41.887
<v Rohan>Sixty times sixty... three thousand six hundred seconds in an hour!

00:00:41.887 --> 00:00:45.110
<v Mishu>Exactly. Now, the railway station.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:48.897
<v Rohan>Train at fifteen thirty hours? There's no fifteen o'clock!

00:00:48.897 --> 00:00:52.772
<v Ishita>Take away twelve. It's three thirty in the afternoon!

00:00:52.772 --> 00:00:57.040
<v Mishu>That's the twenty-four-hour clock. No a.m.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:02.562
<v Mishu>or p.m., and no time repeats in a day, so trains and planes never get mixed up.

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:05.726
<v Rohan>Fifteen thirty. I'm not late for that one!
